\subsection{Progress Display}

While the download proceeds, users need feedback showing that progress is being
made. A silent client leaves users wondering whether it's working, stuck, or
crashed. We display progress updates every two seconds, balancing information
density against terminal spam.

\subsubsection{What the User Sees}

During an active download, the client outputs lines like this to stderr:

\begin{verbatim}
2024-01-15T10:32:15  INFO 42/100 pieces (42.0%) - 1.5 MB/s - ETA: 3m 28s
2024-01-15T10:32:17  INFO 44/100 pieces (44.0%) - 1.8 MB/s - ETA: 3m 12s
2024-01-15T10:32:19  INFO 47/100 pieces (47.0%) - 2.1 MB/s - ETA: 2m 45s
\end{verbatim}

Each progress line contains four pieces of information:

\begin{description}
\item[Piece count] The number of verified pieces out of the total (e.g.,
  [[42/100 pieces]]). This is the most reliable measure of progress because
  pieces are only counted after SHA1 verification.

\item[Percentage] The completion percentage, derived from the piece count.
  For a 100-piece torrent at 42 verified pieces, this shows [[42.0\%]].

\item[Download speed] The current download speed in human-readable units.
  We show B/s, KB/s, MB/s, or GB/s depending on magnitude. This is calculated
  from bytes downloaded since the last update, providing a responsive measure
  of current performance rather than a sluggish average.

\item[Estimated time remaining] A prediction of how long until completion,
  based on current speed and remaining bytes. Displayed as [[5m 30s]] for
  short durations, [[2h 15m 30s]] for longer ones, or [[calculating...]] when
  speed data is insufficient.
\end{description}

\subsubsection{Implementation Strategy}

The progress display is implemented in the event loop (Chapter~7), not in the
CLI module. This is because progress information comes from the download
state---the [[PieceManager]] knows how many pieces are verified, and the
[[EventLoopContext]] tracks bytes downloaded.

The CLI module's responsibility is limited to:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Parsing the [[{-}{-}verbose]] flag to select log level
\item Initializing the logging subsystem with [[init\_logging]]
\item Directing log output to stderr (keeping stdout clean for potential
  machine-readable output)
\end{enumerate}

The [[tracing]] crate handles the actual formatting, including timestamps.
By using [[tracing::info!]] for progress messages, we get consistent formatting
with other log messages and allow users to filter progress with [[RUST\_LOG]]:

\begin{verbatim}
# Suppress progress messages, show only warnings
RUST_LOG=warn literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Speed Calculation}

Download speed is calculated from the change in verified bytes between updates:

\begin{equation}
\text{speed} = \frac{\text{bytes\_now} - \text{bytes\_last}}{\text{time\_now} - \text{time\_last}}
\end{equation}

This ``instantaneous'' approach responds quickly to speed changes---if your
connection slows down, you see it within 2--4 seconds. The alternative,
average speed since download start, would take minutes to reflect changes
and would be misleading for downloads that started slowly then sped up.

The helper functions [[format\_speed]] and [[format\_duration]] (defined in
Chapter~7) handle unit conversion. For speed:

\begin{itemize}
\item Below 1024 B/s: show as bytes per second (e.g., [[500 B/s]])
\item 1 KB/s to 1 MB/s: show as kilobytes per second (e.g., [[1.5 KB/s]])
\item 1 MB/s to 1 GB/s: show as megabytes per second (e.g., [[1.5 MB/s]])
\item Above 1 GB/s: show as gigabytes per second (e.g., [[1.2 GB/s]])
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Completion Message}

When the download finishes, we display a final summary:

\begin{verbatim}
2024-01-15T10:35:43  INFO Download complete!
2024-01-15T10:35:43  INFO Total: 100/100 pieces in 3m 28s (average 2.3 MB/s)
\end{verbatim}

This confirms successful completion and provides the total download time and
average speed for the entire transfer.

\subsection{Graceful Shutdown}

BitTorrent clients should shut down gracefully when the user presses Ctrl+C.
Abrupt termination causes several problems:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The tracker is not notified, leaving our IP in the peer list until the
  announce interval expires (typically 30 minutes to 2 hours).

\item Peer connections are dropped mid-transfer, which is impolite and may
  cause peers to temporarily blacklist our IP.

\item Partial writes may not be flushed to disk, though this is mitigated by
  operating system buffering.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{The Two-Press Pattern}

We implement a two-press shutdown pattern common in well-behaved CLI tools:

\begin{description}
\item[First Ctrl+C] Initiates graceful shutdown. The client prints
  [[Shutting down...]] and begins the cleanup sequence: notifying the tracker,
  closing peer connections, and flushing disk writes.

\item[Second Ctrl+C] Forces immediate exit. If graceful shutdown is stuck
  (perhaps the tracker is unreachable or a peer isn't responding), the user
  can press Ctrl+C again to exit immediately. This respects user intent while
  giving graceful shutdown a chance.
\end{description}

The forced exit uses exit code 130, which is the standard Unix convention for
termination by SIGINT (128 + signal number, where SIGINT is signal 2).

\subsubsection{What the User Sees}

\begin{verbatim}
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent
2024-01-15T10:32:15  INFO Connecting to tracker...
2024-01-15T10:32:16  INFO Connected to 12 peers
2024-01-15T10:32:17  INFO 5/100 pieces (5.0%) - 1.2 MB/s - ETA: 6m 20s
^C
Shutting down...
2024-01-15T10:32:20  INFO Sending stopped event to tracker...
2024-01-15T10:32:21  INFO Disconnecting from 12 peers...
2024-01-15T10:32:21  INFO Shutdown complete.
\end{verbatim}

If the user is impatient:

\begin{verbatim}
^C
Shutting down...
^C
Forced exit.
$ echo $?
130
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Implementation Architecture}

Signal handling is implemented using Tokio's [[tokio::signal::ctrl\_c()]]
function, which provides an async-friendly way to wait for SIGINT. The actual
implementation lives in Chapter~7 (The Client) because it requires access to
the event loop and client state.

The architecture separates concerns:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{ShutdownController}: A thread-safe struct using atomic variables
  that tracks shutdown state. The signal handler sets a flag; the event loop
  polls it.

\item \textbf{Signal handler}: An async task that waits for Ctrl+C and calls
  [[request\_shutdown()]] on the controller.

\item \textbf{Event loop}: Checks [[is\_shutdown\_requested()]] on each
  iteration and initiates cleanup when true.

\item \textbf{Cleanup sequence}: Sends [[event=stopped]] to the tracker,
  closes peer connections, flushes disk writes, then exits.
\end{enumerate}

This separation ensures the signal handler (which runs in an interrupt-like
context) does minimal work---it just sets a flag. The actual cleanup happens
in the normal event loop context where we have full access to async I/O.

\subsection{Usage Examples}

This section demonstrates typical command-line invocations for common use cases.

\subsubsection{Basic Download}

The simplest invocation requires only the torrent file:

\begin{verbatim}
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu-22.04-desktop-amd64.iso.torrent
\end{verbatim}

This downloads to the current directory, uses port 6881 for incoming connections,
and logs at INFO level. For most users, this is all they need.

\subsubsection{Specifying Output Directory}

To download to a specific location:

\begin{verbatim}
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent -o ~/Downloads
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent --output /media/storage/ISOs
\end{verbatim}

The output directory is created if it doesn't exist. Nested paths like
[[/media/storage/torrents/2024/linux]] are created recursively.

\subsubsection{Custom Port}

If port 6881 is in use or blocked by a firewall:

\begin{verbatim}
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent -p 51413
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent --port 8999
\end{verbatim}

Ports 6881--6889 are traditionally used by BitTorrent clients. If you're behind
a NAT, you may need to configure port forwarding for the chosen port.

\subsubsection{Verbose Logging}

For troubleshooting connection or protocol issues:

\begin{verbatim}
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent -v
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent --verbose
\end{verbatim}

This enables DEBUG-level logging, showing:

\begin{itemize}
\item Individual peer connection attempts and results
\item Protocol message exchanges (handshakes, bitfields, requests)
\item Piece verification successes and failures
\item Tracker request and response details
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Fine-Grained Log Control}

For even more control, use the [[RUST\_LOG]] environment variable:

\begin{verbatim}
# Show only warnings and errors
$ RUST_LOG=warn literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent

# Debug logging for tracker module only
$ RUST_LOG=literate_bittorrent::tracker=debug literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent

# Trace-level logging for pieces module
$ RUST_LOG=literate_bittorrent::pieces=trace literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent
\end{verbatim}

The [[RUST\_LOG]] syntax follows [[tracing-subscriber]]'s [[EnvFilter]]:

\begin{itemize}
\item [[error]], [[warn]], [[info]], [[debug]], [[trace]]: Set global level
\item [[module::path=level]]: Set level for specific module
\item [[module::path]]: Enable all logs for module (shorthand for [[=trace]])
\item Multiple directives separated by commas: [[warn,literate\_bittorrent::tracker=debug]]
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Combining Options}

Options can be combined in any order:

\begin{verbatim}
$ literate-bittorrent -v -o ~/Downloads -p 51413 ubuntu.torrent
$ literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent -o ~/Downloads -v -p 51413
$ literate-bittorrent --verbose --output ~/Downloads --port 51413 ubuntu.torrent
\end{verbatim}

All three commands are equivalent.

\subsubsection{Viewing Help}

For a complete list of options and their descriptions:

\begin{verbatim}
$ literate-bittorrent --help
\end{verbatim}

This displays:

\begin{verbatim}
A pedagogical BitTorrent client implemented as a literate program.

Usage: literate-bittorrent [OPTIONS] <TORRENT>

Arguments:
  <TORRENT>  Path to the .torrent file to download

Options:
  -o, --output <DIR>   Output directory for downloaded files [default: .]
  -p, --port <PORT>    Listen port for incoming peer connections [default: 6881]
  -v, --verbose        Enable verbose (DEBUG level) logging
  -h, --help           Print help
  -V, --version        Print version
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Scripting and Automation}

For scripting, note that:

\begin{itemize}
\item Exit code 0 indicates successful download
\item Exit code 1 indicates an error (invalid arguments, file not found, etc.)
\item Exit code 130 indicates Ctrl+C termination
\item Progress messages go to stderr, keeping stdout clean
\end{itemize}

Example script checking completion:

\begin{verbatim}
#!/bin/bash
if literate-bittorrent ubuntu.torrent -o /backup 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "Download complete"
else
    echo "Download failed with exit code $?"
fi
\end{verbatim}
